This file applies to MAGI runs. Ignore it in CONCLAVE (`/conclave`, `camerlengo-8`) and unrelated chats.

For native Cursor mode, declare the route with the existing `magi-whoami` (`cursor`) check. For `cursor-cli`, use the runtime-local command below. These checks declare the route, not the actual host model. For operational status, use a current check as described in [live-check.mdc](live-check.mdc).

## Arbiter boundary

- The Cursor arbiter is xAI Grok only. It is not a MAGI seat.
- The arbiter classifies, prepares plans and briefs, launches seats, and requests the mode's evidence and deterministic completion checks.
- The arbiter MUST NOT implement, review, verify, repair, or vote on product artifacts. If it does, activation fails.
- Deterministic checks outrank the arbiter and every seat. The arbiter cannot waive a failing code/schema/proof/rules check.

## Cursor CLI only

For `hostMode: cursor-cli` or `/magi-cli`, use the [MAGI CLI skill](../skills/magi-cli/SKILL.md) and its [run procedure](../skills/magi-cli/references/cursor-cli.md). Its dispatch matrix selects legal vendor/model/effort combinations by role and class. Grok 4.6 is the non-voting arbiter; substantive planning and research also belong to vendor seats.

From the runtime root, run `node tools/magi-whoami.js --mode cursor-cli --slug grok-4.6`. Stop unless it returns `LEGAL` with exit 0. This validates the declaration against the runtime matrix; it does not prove the actual picker.

Before the first production dispatch:

1. Classify every unit. Set `MAGI_RULES_ROOT` to the external STANDING v2 / R01-R22 pack and run the CLI preflight.
   Set `MAGI_CAPACITY_RECEIPT` and `MAGI_LEGACY_CAPACITY` to current evidence-backed files before native calls. Admission checks run in the probe and dispatch entrypoints. Read the maintained `seat-profiles.json` operational lessons for arbiter duties; the runner inserts selected leaf procedures into contracts.
2. Probe every selected exact vendor/model/effort pair with `model-probe.js --vendor <vendor> --model <model> --effort <effort> --evidence-dir <new-probe-dir>`. Import each captured `probe.json` with `model-availability.js --file <availability.json> --probe <probe.json>`.
3. Require actual native observed identity and hashed evidence for every route. Unknown, mismatched, or unproven pairs are unavailable. Evidence expires 60 minutes after its original completion; importing it again does not renew it.
4. Finalize the briefs and their SHA256 hashes. Prepare the whole plan with `planId`, `hostMode: "cursor-cli"`, `arbiter: {vendor: "xai", model: "grok-4.6", effort: "high"}`, `magiConvened`, and all `dispatches`. Arbiter effort supports `low`, `medium`, `high`, and `xhigh`; `high` is the default. Fast mode is optional.
5. Each dispatch binds `dispatchId`, `unitId`, `class`, `role`, `vendor`, `model`, `effort`, absolute `cwd`, absolute `brief`, `briefSha256`, and relative `writeScope`. Review/verify entries require correct foreign `authorVendor`. Read-only roles have `writeScope: []`. Use the matrix class `codingDefault` for coding and record `routingReason` for nondefault OpenAI coding selections. Read the CLI coding selection guide. Carry escalation authorization where the selected benchmark or noncoding route still requires it.
6. Seal the complete plan with `node <runtime-root>/tools/plan-seal.js --plan <draft.json> --run-dir <new-run-dir> --availability <availability.json>`. A policy failure stops the launch.
7. Launch each entry through `node <runtime-root>/tools/dispatch-run.js --plan <run-dir/dispatch-plan.json> --run-dir <run-dir> --dispatch-id <id> --rules-root <external-v2-pack>`. The runtime binds the selected entry, staged skill/rule hashes, native proof, scope audit, receipts, and idempotent telemetry.

Launch uses the availability evidence pinned by the seal. Omit `--availability` on `dispatch-run`; that optional argument accepts only a byte-identical copy for relocation. A refreshed availability snapshot requires a new complete plan and seal.

Only the arbiter owns orchestration, routing, bridge, distribution, assessment, and retrospective skills. Leaves receive their selected vendor card and role/class skills. They cannot delegate or edit policy, receipts, evidence, or telemetry. Non-implementation roles stay read-only under their native profile.

Convened implementation requires at least `min(3, implementation unit count)` distinct implementation vendors. Every plan with at least two implementation units has the matrix's 60% maximum vendor share. Plans without implementation do not manufacture implementation rows. Preserve all class, author-independence, and critical-panel requirements.

Project slices and graphs define units, dependencies, and write scope. A SLICES vendor column or `graph.json` `vendorOverride` grants no CLI route authority. Changing a route, class, author, brief, or scope requires a revised complete plan, all policy checks, and a new seal.

Finalize with `run-finalize.js --run-dir <run-dir>` and activate with `activation-check.js --run-dir <run-dir>`. Use `panel-tally.js --run-dir <run-dir> --unit-id <unit>` for verified native POSITION votes and author recusal. Execution and approval are separate results. The arbiter cannot fabricate votes or telemetry, hand-count passage, or waive failure.

Resolve `<runtime-root>` from the MAGI source checkout or installed runtime directory. Standalone smoke and transport diagnostics cannot activate production work.

## Native Cursor only

For an explicitly selected `hostMode: cursor` run, follow the [native MAGI skill](../skills/magi/SKILL.md) and [native Task model table](../skills/magi/references/cursor-host.md). Their native routing, distribution, and tally policy is scoped to that mode and cannot override the CLI branch above.

If a native host check switches a dispatch to `cursor-cli`, use the CLI skill and validate a complete sealed CLI plan before launch.
